steps.message:
Do not register IProductData twice, and do not constructor-inject a
transient typed client into singleton ProductData.

**Option A — singleton facade with a named client factory:**

```csharp
builder.Services.AddHttpClient("products", client =>
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com/"));
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IProductData, ProductData>();
```

```csharp
public sealed class ProductData(IHttpClientFactory clients) : IProductData
{
    readonly Dictionary<int, Product> _cache = [];

    public async Task<Product> GetAsync(int id)
    {
        if (_cache.TryGetValue(id, out var cached))
            return cached;

        using HttpClient client = clients.CreateClient("products");
        Product product =
            (await client.GetFromJsonAsync<Product>($"products/{id}"))!;
        _cache[id] = product;
        return product;
    }
}
```

**Option B — transient typed consumer with a separate singleton cache:**

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IProductCache, ProductCache>();
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<IProductData, ProductData>();

public sealed class ProductData(
    HttpClient client,
    IProductCache cache) : IProductData
{
}
```

In option A, the singleton creates a named client per operation. In
option B, ProductData is transient and the separate cache owns
app-wide state. Neither option pins a typed client in a singleton.
